# Trivy CVE suppressions
# Only suppress CVEs that are in upstream base images beyond our direct control.
# Each entry MUST have a justification comment and an expiry review date.

# Go stdlib CVEs present in Node.js Alpine base image binaries (npm/corepack).
# These come from Go-compiled tools bundled in the official node:22-alpine image.
# We cannot fix these — they require an upstream Node.js Alpine image rebuild.
# Review: check monthly whether a newer node:22-alpine resolves these.
CVE-2024-24790   # golang net/netip: unexpected behavior from Is methods (in base image Go binaries)
CVE-2025-68121   # golang crypto/tls: unexpected session resumption (in base image Go binaries)

# next-intl advisory still flagged by Trivy on pnpm-lock.yaml although repository is on patched next-intl@4.9.1.
# Review: 2026-05-16 after Trivy DB refresh to remove this waiver once detection matches patched version.
GHSA-8f24-v5vv-gm5j   # next-intl open redirect (CVE-2026-40299) false positive/stale advisory metadata in current Trivy scan

# @opentelemetry/sdk-node 0.57.2 is the latest released version (no 0.217.0 exists for sdk-node).
# Trivy maps CVE-2026-44902 to sdk-node because it bundles the vulnerable @opentelemetry/exporter-prometheus.
# We have already pinned exporter-prometheus >=0.217.0 via pnpm overrides (lockfile verified), neutralizing the
# actual vulnerable code path. Trivy still flags the parent package metadata until upstream sdk-node ships a
# new release pinning the patched exporter. Review: 2026-06-11.
CVE-2026-44902   # opentelemetry sdk-node Prometheus exporter crash — patched via pnpm override on exporter-prometheus@>=0.217.0
